    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-size-means" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Size Means</a>
          <a href="#sizing-by-job" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Sizing by Job</a>
          <a href="#bigger-isnt-better" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Why Bigger Is Not Better</a>
          <a href="#hose-diameter" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Hose Diameter</a>
          <a href="#wet-pickup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sizing for Water</a>
          <a href="#matching-to-tools" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Matching to Power Tools</a>
          <a href="#picking-yours" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Picking Yours</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A sixteen-gallon shop vac with a weak motor and a narrow hose will lose to a six-gallon machine with strong airflow on nearly every job. Tank size tells you how often you stop to empty. It tells you almost nothing about how well the thing picks up.
        </p>

        <div id="what-size-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What “Size” Actually Means on a Shop Vac
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vacs are sold on gallons, and gallons describe the tank rather than the machine. A 16-gallon vac with a weak motor and a 1.25-inch hose will underperform a 6-gallon unit with strong airflow and a 2.5-inch hose on almost every job, because tank volume decides how often you stop to empty, not how well the thing picks up.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are really three independent specifications hiding behind the word size. Tank capacity decides emptying frequency and how much water you can lift at once. Airflow, measured in cubic feet per minute, decides how well it picks up bulky debris. Hose diameter decides what can pass through without jamming, and it constrains airflow more than most buyers realise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get all three right and the machine feels effortless. Get the gallons right and the other two wrong and you own a large drum that struggles.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Sizing Rule Worth Remembering</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the tank for how often you want to stop, and the hose for what you plan to pick up.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Tank size is a convenience specification. Hose diameter and airflow are performance specifications. Most people over-buy the first and under-buy the second.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="sizing-by-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Sizing by the Job You Actually Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the capacities that suit real tasks, based on how much debris each one generates before you would want to empty the tank.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tank Size</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Realistic Limits</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2-4 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car interiors, craft rooms, small spills</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fills fast on any real debris</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5-8 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home garage, light DIY, one room at a time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The most common sensible size</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">9-14 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Workshops, renovation cleanup, wet pickup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy but rarely needs emptying mid-job</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">16-20 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Job sites, flooded basements, sawdust volume</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Too heavy to carry full; needs floor space</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20+ gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contractor and commercial use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Overkill for a domestic garage</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            For most households the honest answer is 5 to 8 gallons. It holds enough that ordinary jobs finish without a trip to the bin, it is light enough to carry up stairs and lift into a car boot, and it stores in a corner rather than occupying a parking space.
          </p>

          <p>
            The step up to 9 to 14 gallons is worth it if you regularly cut wood, renovate, or expect to deal with water. A wet pickup fills a tank far faster than debris does — water is heavy and the tank fills by volume, not by weight — and having to stop and drain a small tank four times mid-flood is genuinely miserable.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="bigger-isnt-better" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Bigger Is Not Automatically Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 16-gallon shop vac full of water weighs well over a hundred pounds. You will not carry it, tip it into a sink, or take it up a flight of stairs, which means the size that sounded generous in the shop becomes a machine you drain with a hose and move on castors only.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and Manoeuvrability</h3>
          <p>
            Small tanks get carried; large tanks get dragged. If your work happens in one place — a garage, a workshop — dragging is fine. If you carry the vac between a car, a basement and a spare bedroom, a 5 to 8 gallon unit you can pick up with one hand changes how often you actually use it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage Footprint</h3>
          <p>
            A 16-gallon vac with its hose stored on board occupies roughly the same floor area as a small filing cabinet, permanently. In a single-car garage already holding bicycles and a workbench, that matters more than the two extra gallons.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Filter Area, Not Tank Volume, Sets Run Time on Fine Dust</h3>
          <p>
            For sanding and drywall work, the machine stops performing when the filter blinds, not when the tank fills. A big tank with a small cartridge will lose suction long before it is full, which is why a bag and a large-surface-area pleated filter matter more than capacity on dusty jobs.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            There is also a hidden cost to a tank you empty rarely. Debris left sitting in a shop vac — damp leaves, wet sawdust, ash — turns sour within days and the smell transfers to everything the machine exhausts afterwards. Owners of large tanks empty less often precisely because they can, and the tank becomes a container rather than a pass-through. A smaller tank enforces a habit that keeps the machine pleasant to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other consideration is the height of the unit. Tall, narrow tanks tip more easily when a stiff hose is pulled sideways, which is how a full vac ends up on its side across a garage floor. Shorter, wider tanks in the same capacity are noticeably more stable, and stability matters more the heavier the machine is.
          </p>

        <div id="hose-diameter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Hose Diameter Matters More Than the Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            The hose is the part of a shop vac people ignore and then complain about. It sets both what fits through and how much air the machine can actually move.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hose Diameter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Airflow</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.25 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car detailing, crevices, fine dust only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.875 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General household and garage debris</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2.5 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wood chips, leaves, water, renovation debris</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            A 2.5-inch hose moves substantially more air than a 1.25-inch one on the same motor, and it swallows debris that would jam the smaller hose repeatedly. The trade is bulk and stiffness — a 2.5-inch hose is awkward for detailing a car footwell, which is exactly what the small hose is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer is a machine with a 2.5-inch port and an adapter or reducer set, so you get full airflow for heavy work and a narrow tip when you need reach. Most mid-size vacs ship this way; check before buying, because a machine locked to a 1.875-inch port limits what you can do later.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="wet-pickup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing for Wet Pickup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water changes the arithmetic completely. A gallon of water weighs a little over eight pounds, so a 12-gallon tank filled with water holds roughly a hundred pounds of liquid — well past what anyone lifts and tips.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical points follow. First, size the tank for how much water you expect to remove in one go: a dishwasher leak is a few gallons, a flooded basement corner after a storm can be dozens. Second, choose a model with a drain port near the base. A drain plug and a garden hose turn emptying into a ninety-second job; without one, you are tipping a heavy drum into a floor drain and wearing some of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never fill past the maximum water line marked on the tank. Above that level the float shut-off cannot do its job properly and water can reach the motor housing. The float is a safety device, not a convenience feature, and machines without one need watching closely.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="matching-to-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching a Shop Vac to Power Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the vac will be connected to a sander, a track saw or a router for dust extraction, two features matter more than size.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">An Auto-Start Outlet</h3>
          <p>
            A tool-triggered outlet starts the vac when the tool draws current and runs it on for a couple of seconds afterwards to clear the hose. It sounds like a small convenience and it changes how much you actually use extraction, because you stop having to switch two machines on and off for every cut.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adequate Airflow With a Narrow Hose</h3>
          <p>
            Tool ports are small, typically 1.25 to 1.5 inches, so the machine has to move usable air through a restrictive path. This is where a cheap vac with an inflated horsepower claim disappoints — the number on the box comes from an unrestricted motor test, not from pulling air through a sander shroud.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Filter Cleaning</h3>
          <p>
            Sanding dust blinds a cartridge quickly, and suction falls off within minutes on a fine-dust job. Dedicated dust extractors solve this with automatic filter pulsing; shop vacs solve it with a bag plus a habit of tapping or cleaning the filter between sessions. Either works; ignoring it does not.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One practical trick for wet work with a small tank: position the vac lower than the water you are removing wherever the layout allows, and run a longer hose rather than moving the machine. Lifting water costs suction, so a vac standing at the bottom of basement steps pulling from the floor beside it will clear a puddle noticeably faster than the same machine sitting at the top of the stairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about where the water goes. Emptying twelve gallons into a laundry sink is a slow, splashy job even with a drain port; running a garden hose from the drain to a floor drain or out of a door is not. If flood cleanup is a realistic use case for you, plan the disposal route before you buy the tank size, because that is the part that determines whether the job takes twenty minutes or an hour.
          </p>

        <div id="picking-yours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Putting It Together: Picking Your Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical house with a garage and occasional DIY, a 6 to 9 gallon machine with a 2.5-inch hose, a drain port and a fine dust filter option covers essentially everything, stores in a corner and can still be carried to a car.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a workshop where the vac lives in one place and handles sawdust and extraction, step up to 12 to 16 gallons and put it on castors. The weight stops mattering when the machine does not travel, and the larger tank means fewer interruptions during a long session.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an apartment or a car-focused user, stay at 2 to 5 gallons with a 1.25-inch hose and a good crevice tool. The small hose that would frustrate a woodworker is exactly right for seat rails and door pockets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the size, treat the accessories as part of the purchase rather than an afterthought. A floor brush, a crevice tool, a wide utility nozzle and a bag pack cost very little and decide how often the machine gets used. Our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac comparison</a> covers which models include useful heads and which ship with a token bag of plastic.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Gallons Instead of Airflow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tank capacity is the number on the front of the box and the least predictive of performance. Airflow in CFM, sealed suction in inches of water lift, and hose diameter decide how the machine actually picks up. A big drum with a weak motor is a common and disappointing purchase.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Tank You Cannot Lift When Full</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gallon of water weighs over eight pounds, so a twelve-gallon tank of water is around a hundred pounds. If the machine has no drain port, emptying it means tipping that weight into a drain. Either buy a drain port or buy a size you can actually handle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Hose Diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1.25-inch hose jams repeatedly on wood chips and leaves and strangles airflow on any bulk pickup. If the machine will do garage and workshop work, insist on a 2.5-inch port and use reducers for detail work rather than the other way around.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Big Tank to Solve Fine Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On sanding and drywall work the machine stops performing when the filter blinds, long before the tank is full. Capacity does nothing for that — a collection bag and a large pleated fine dust cartridge do. Size the filtration for dusty work, not the drum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Past the Maximum Water Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The float shut-off is a safety device that needs headroom to work. Filling above the marked line risks water reaching the motor housing. On machines without a float, watch the level yourself and drain early rather than testing where the limit is.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size shop vac do I need for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Five to eight gallons suits most households. It holds enough that ordinary garage and DIY jobs finish without emptying, it is light enough to carry up stairs or into a car, and it stores in a corner. Step up to nine to fourteen gallons if you cut wood regularly, renovate, or expect to pick up water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a bigger shop vac always better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Tank size decides how often you stop to empty, not how well the machine picks up — airflow and hose diameter do that. A large tank full of water can weigh over a hundred pounds, cannot be lifted or tipped, and occupies permanent floor space. Many people would be better served by a smaller tank and a wider hose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size shop vac do I need for water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the tank to the volume you expect to remove: a few gallons for an appliance leak, twelve or more for a flooded basement corner. More important than capacity is a drain port near the base, which lets you empty with a garden hose instead of tipping. Never fill past the marked maximum water line.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does hose size matter on a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                More than tank size does. A 2.5-inch hose moves considerably more air than a 1.25-inch hose on the same motor and swallows debris that would jam the narrow one. The small hose is better for car interiors and crevices. The ideal setup is a 2.5-inch port with reducers so you can use both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many gallons is a good shop vac for a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Six to nine gallons is the sweet spot for a domestic garage. It handles sawdust, leaves, the car, and a modest spill without needing emptying mid-job, and it remains light enough to move easily. Sixteen-gallon machines suit workshops and job sites where the vac stays in one place on castors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do I need for connecting a shop vac to power tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A tool-triggered auto-start outlet, adapters to fit your tools' dust ports, and a plan for filter clogging. Sanding dust blinds a cartridge within minutes, so use a collection bag and clean or tap the filter between sessions. Dedicated dust extractors automate that with filter pulsing, which is the main reason they cost more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does tank size affect suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at all directly. Suction comes from the motor, the impeller and how restricted the air path is; tank volume only determines how long you can work before emptying. A small vac with strong airflow and a wide hose outperforms a large vac with a weak motor and a narrow hose on every pickup task.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

