          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes, <strong class="text-white">six to nine gallons with a 2.5-inch hose and a drain port</strong> is the right answer. It is big enough that ordinary jobs finish without emptying, small enough to carry, and wide enough at the hose to swallow what a garage produces.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Go larger only if the machine will live in one place on castors. Go smaller only if cars and crevices are the main use. And in every case, <strong class="text-white">buy the hose and the filtration deliberately</strong> — they decide performance in a way that gallons never will.
          </p>
